{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 asserts that independent failures follow a Poisson distribution and shared-cause failures exhibit temporal synchronicity, but this mechanism confuses the symptom (temporal clustering) with the cause. The problem demands a causal mechanism that distinguishes truly independent failures from merely-appearing-uncorrelated ones. Poisson-distributed arrival times can still produce coincidental bursts that mimic synchronicity, and, conversely, a shared latent cause (e.g., a slow-propagating misconfiguration) can produce staggered, non-synchronous failures that look independent. The countercondition about cascade propagation partially concedes this, but the core mechanism still fails to provide the causal evidence the problem requires because it relies on statistical pattern-matching rather than establishing an actual causal link to a shared root cause.","counterexample":["A leap-second bug triggers kernel hangs in geographically dispersed nodes, but because each node's NTP sync drifts by seconds to minutes, the resulting failures are staggered rather than synchronous—appearing Poisson-distributed despite sharing a single root cause."],"target":"73adfb74d770147be36b9e76f4208fb9352b227752eb0be58392e053c2963a9a"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 conflates organizational decentralization with technical heterogeneity. The claim asserts that the network replaces 'homogeneous redundancy with operational heterogeneity,' but a decentralized network of independently operated services can still be entirely homogeneous in implementation (same framework, same OS, same logic path). The mechanism assumes heterogeneity exists as a precondition, yet the problem statement concerns a decentralized network vs. a monolith with strictly more internal redundancy. If the decentralized nodes share a software monoculture, the 'biological firewall' mechanism fails entirely, meaning the argument for greater resilience does not hold without establishing that heterogeneity is actually guaranteed.","counterexample":["A decentralized network of 10 microservices all built on the same Node.js framework and deployed on the same Alpine Linux base image, where a single zlib memory corruption bug causes 100% penetration across all nodes—matching the monolith's failure mode without providing the promised heterogeneity-based resilience."],"target":"8f9fc7110d19998081d2d0523b3e1aed444cb491d41fcc9c5e07d2029e032cdb"}]}